            <ArticleTitle>Economic burden of underweight and overweight among adults in the Asia-Pacific region: a systematic review.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>458-69</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/tmi.12679</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess the economic burden of underweight and overweight among adults in the Asia-Pacific region.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">Systematic review of articles published until March 2015.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Seventeen suitable articles were found, of which 13 assess the economic burden of overweight/obesity and estimate that it accounts for 1.5-9.9% of a country's total healthcare expenditure. Four articles on the economic burden of underweight estimate it at 2.5-3.8% of the country's total GDP. Using hospital data, and compared to normal weight individuals, four articles estimated extra healthcare costs for overweight individuals of 7-9.8% and more, and extra healthcare costs for obese individuals of 17-22.3% and higher.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Despite methodological diversity across the studies, there is a consensus that both underweight and overweight impose a substantial financial burden on healthcare systems in the Asia-Pacific region.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2016 John Wiley &amp; Sons Ltd.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
